Here's a plan for generalizing this from a flat one-level graph to a --level N hierarchical one.

Core idea
Right now a "node" is just a first-level folder name (a flat string). To support levels, a node needs to become a path of folder names, truncated to at most N components — e.g. at --level 2, a file under moduleA/sub1/foo.c maps to node id "moduleA/sub1", while a file directly under moduleA/foo.c (no subdirectory) maps to "moduleA".

Whether a folder is drawn as a plain node or exploded into a subgraph cluster depends purely on the real directory structure, not on where dependencies point: a first-level folder becomes a cluster_* subgraph (containing its immediate children as nodes) if and only if it has actual subdirectories on disk and --level allows going that deep.

Proposed changes, by piece
1. Folder-tree discovery (new, replaces the flat first_level_subfolders)
Walk the input root recursively up to depth N, building a tree:

￼

rust
struct FolderNode {
    path_id: String,             // e.g. "moduleA/sub1", used as the dot node/cluster id
    children: Vec<FolderNode>,   // non-empty only if this folder has subdirs AND depth < N
}
This is purely filesystem-driven — independent of any .d file content.

2. classify() becomes tree-aware
Instead of matching a flat list of names, it walks the FolderNode tree: find the first path component matching a root FolderNode's name (same left-to-right scan as today), then keep descending into children as long as the next path component matches a child name. It stops at whichever node it bottoms out at — either a leaf FolderNode, or a folder whose next component didn't match any known child (see open question below).

3. Edge collection & transitive reduction — unchanged
edges_from_pairs, transitive_reduction, and the red-bidirectional-edge logic all operate on generic (String, String) node-id pairs already. Since node ids are now just longer strings ("moduleA/sub1" instead of "moduleA"), none of that logic needs to change.

4. render_dot() needs real subgraph support

Leaf FolderNodes render as plain "path_id"; declarations, as today.
FolderNodes with children render as:
￼

  subgraph cluster_moduleA {
      label="moduleA";
      "moduleA/sub1";
      "moduleA/sub2";
  }
recursively, for arbitrarily deep nesting.

Edges are just written as before ("A/sub1" -> "B") — Graphviz natively draws edges crossing cluster boundaries correctly without any extra syntax, since the edge references real nodes, not the cluster itself.
Cluster names need sanitizing (/ and other characters aren't valid in a Graphviz ID, so cluster_moduleA_sub1 or similar).
5. CLI
Add --level N (default 1, so existing behavior is preserved exactly at the default). Values beyond the tree's actual depth just have no further effect (recursion stops naturally when a folder has no more subdirectories).

