Good distinction — and it's a genuinely different algorithm, not just a rendering choice. Let me lay out how both modes share a foundation, then how they diverge.

Shared foundation: full ancestor chains
Instead of classify() returning just the deepest matched node, it should return the whole chain of matched ancestors:

rust
fn classify_chain(raw_path: &str, tree: &[FolderNode]) -> Option<Vec<String>>
For A/sub1/subsub1/file.c (with --level 3 and that structure existing), this returns ["A", "A/sub1", "A/sub1/subsub1"]. It still returns None in exactly the cases we already agreed on: no match at all (out of tree), or a folder that's exploded but the file sits directly in it outside any recognized child.

Both modes consume this chain — they just pick different endpoints from it.

Mode A — flat
Endpoint = the last element of each chain: target_chain.last(), prereq_chain.last(). This is exactly what I described before — deepest-known-node-to-deepest-known-node, regardless of how far apart they are in the tree.

Mode B — hierarchical
Endpoint = the first point of divergence between the two chains:

Walk both chains together, index by index, while they're equal — this is the common ancestor prefix.
Where they first differ (or one runs out — see note below), the edge is drawn between target_chain[d] and prereq_chain[d] at that index d.
If the chains are identical all the way (same finest node on both sides), it's an internal same-node dependency — dropped, same as today's same-folder rule.
Checking against your two examples:

A/sub1/file → B/sub2/file: chains ["A","A/sub1"] vs ["B","B/sub2"] diverge at index 0 → edge A -> B. ✓.
A/subA1/file → A/subA2/file: chains ["A","A/subA1"] vs ["A","A/subA2"] diverge at index 1 → edge A/subA1 -> A/subA2, i.e. inside cluster A. ✓.
(Aside: chains can never be a strict prefix of one another — if A had a child, any file "directly in A" would already have been dropped by the Option-B rule, so this divergence-or-identical dichotomy is exhaustive; no edge case there.)

How this plugs into the rest
edges_from_pairs just needs a mode parameter and picks Mode A's or Mode B's endpoint-selection logic per pair.
transitive_reduction and the red bidirectional-edge merge need no changes at all — since every node id is a globally unique path string regardless of which mode produced it, the whole edge set (mixing top-level and deeply-nested edges) is still just one consistent graph to reduce/merge, whether flat or hierarchical.
Rendering: worth knowing that Graphviz determines cluster membership purely from where nodes are declared, not where edge statements sit in the file — so correctness doesn't require nesting edge statements inside their subgraph cluster_X {} block. I'd still nest them there for readability of the generated .dot source (an A/sub1 -> A/sub2 edge reads more sensibly sitting next to cluster A's node declarations), but it's cosmetic, not required.


